{"id":"144fd7b3-c949-4e3c-a872-c04599094968","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009902,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    core_distance = case.get('core_distance')\\n    distances = case.get('distances')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError('nodes must be a non-empty list')\\n    if not isinstance(core_distance, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('core_distance must be a dict')\\n    if not isinstance(distances, list):\\n        raise ValueError('distances must be a list')\\n    node_set = set(nodes)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node not in core_distance:\\n            raise ValueError(f'core_distance missing node {node}')\\n    edges = []\\n    for entry in distances:\\n        if len(entry) != 3:\\n            raise ValueError('each distance entry must have 3 elements')\\n        a, b, d = entry\\n        if a not in node_set or b not in node_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f'unknown node in distance {a} or {b}')\\n        if a == b:\\n            continue\\n        # canonicalize undirected endpoints\\n        if a > b:\\n            a, b = b, a\\n        core_a = core_distance[a]\\n        core_b = core_distance[b]\\n        weight = max(core_a, core_b, d)\\n        edges.append((weight, a, b))\\n    # sort by weight, then a, then b (a<b guaranteed)\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    # union-find\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    for weight, a, b in edges:\\n        if union(a, b):\\n            mst_edges.append([a, b, weight])\\n            total_weight += weight\\n            if len(mst_edges) == n - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != n - 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Graph is disconnected, cannot form MST')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python source code for solve(case) that computes the MST of mutual reachability graph. The output must match the schema: an object with \"mst_edges\" (list of [string, string, number]) and \"total_weight\" (number). The case payload has nodes list, core_distance dict mapping node to float, distances list of triples [node1, node2, distance]. We need to compute mutual reachability: max(core_dist[node1], core_dist[node2], distance). Then build graph, compute MST using Kruskal with deterministic tie-breaking: sort edges by mutual_reachability, then by node1, then node2 (strings). Use union-find (disjoint set) to select edges. Must reject disconnected input (i.e., after processing all edges, we need exactly len(nodes)-1 edges? Actually if graph is connected, Kruskal will find enough edges. But if not connected, we won't have enough edges; we should raise ValueError or handle? The spec says \"Reject disconnected inputs and return selected edges plus total weight.\" So if graph is disconnected, we should raise ValueError. Also note: edges are undirected; we must canonicalize endpoints (e.g., sort alphabetically). Also ensure we use exactly nodes from nodes list; distances list might contain all pairs? Should be comprehensive? We'll assume distances cover all pairs? But not necessarily; if missing edges, graph may be disconnected. So we compute mutual reachability for each distance entry (or for each pair of nodes? The distances list is given as edges; we should consider only those provided? The case includes distances for all pairs? In the examples, it's all 10 pairs for 5 nodes. But we shouldn't assume. Compute mutual_reachability for each given triple. Then treat that as an undirected edge with weight. Then run MST. If not enough edges or graph disconnected, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso note: core_distance dict might not have entries for all nodes? But nodes list defines nodes. We'll assume core_distance has all nodes; if not, raise error.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Validate input: nodes list non-empty, core_distance keys cover nodes, distances list each has 3 elements, node1 and node2 in nodes, distance number.\n- Build edges list: for each [a,b,d] in distances, canonicalize (a,b) as sorted pair. Compute core_a, core_b from core_distance. weight = max(core_a, core_b, d). Append (weight, a, b) to list.\n- Sort edges by weight, then a, then b (string comparison).\n- Initialize union-find for each node (map node to index). Iterate over sorted edges, if find(u) != find(v), union, add edge (a,b,weight) to mst_edges, add weight to total_weight. Stop when mst_edges length == len(nodes)-1.\n- After loop, if mst_edges length != len(nodes)-1, raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\").\n- Return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}.\n\nNeed to ensure deterministic: sort edges by (weight, a, b) with a<b for canonicalization.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires \"mst_edges\" items are arrays of [string, string, number] with exactly 3 elements. The order of endpoints? It doesn't specify order; but canonicalization ensures consistent ordering. The spec says \"Canonicalize undirected endpoints.\" So we output sorted pair: smaller string first.\n\nEdge tie-breaking: \"Sort by reachability then endpoint IDs.\" So yes.\n\nAll code should be in solve(case) function, using only standard library.\n\nNow produce fina